Chapter: RocReturnedClosure
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 the case named "inspect: returned closure calls captured function
 argument". The Roc source is four lines and this is all of it:

     wrap : (I64 -> I64) -> { next : () -> I64 }
     wrap = |transform| { next: || transform(1.I64) }
     wrapped = wrap(|_| 9.I64)
     (wrapped.next)()

 and Roc expects 9.

 One environment adaptation, named here because a reader will otherwise
 take the port for a loose one: Roc's `next` is a THUNK, `() -> I64`, and
 Codex has no nullary function type -- a nullary definition is a constant,
 evaluated once, not a closure to enter. So `next` takes an Integer and
 ignores it. What the case asks survives that. The closure is still built
 inside `wrap`, still captures `wrap`'s function-typed PARAMETER, still
 leaves as a record field, and is still entered through that field after
 `wrap` has returned and its frame is gone.

 That last part is what no row in this corpus asks yet.
 `record-smoke.codex` stores a lambda in a record field, but it captures a
 Text and an Integer. `dce-reach.codex` passes a function as a value, but
 calls it in the frame that received it. Neither captures a FUNCTION in a
 closure that outlives the call which built it -- which is the exact
 neighbourhood of findings 38, 39, 40 and 42, and the reason this file was
 the one worth porting first.

Section: Types

  Wrapped = record {
   next : Integer -> Integer
  }

Section: Functions

  wrap : (Integer -> Integer) -> Wrapped
  wrap (transform) = Wrapped { next = \ignored -> transform 1 }

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   let wrapped = wrap (\x -> 9)
   in print-line-uni (show ((wrapped.next) 0))
  end
